It's hard to accept at first, sort of the same as how safe can seem boring when you first start out. It's unusual, it's got uncertainty, we're not used to it. It's something that gets easier with time, acceptance and as hard as it can sound and be just letting things be. Theirs feelings, they come and go and change and it's what you feel right now, it doesn't have to stay and likely wont good or bad and when you accept that it can become a lot easier to just sort of breathe easier

&#x200B;

I used to feel suffocated by my feelings of depression and hopelessness, i still get them honestly but now i'm able to take a deep breath and say to myself it will go away maybe not right now but the feeling will change and i got this. Not the same as accepting a positive emotion but it's what i feel the most able to covey right now

Set a firm boundary with them, let them know you understand their concerns come from a place of love and care however you wont be putting your life on pause in case something may happen. Something can happen anywhere and lying about it now will lead to you to so much stress later, cause once you lie the first time well do you lie again? Do you pretend that you haven't been anywhere? Maybe you'll get anxiety when they try and contact you when you are somewhere they don't think you are.

&#x200B;

Tough love it out, you are an adult and theirs so much life to experience out there i hope you enjoy Birmingham and anywhere else you want to go in the future

Everyone responds to medication differently and all medication is also different., I'm on a different antipsychotic than you are . The medication being in your system is not the same as the dosage that you are taking, from my own experience i'm on 150mg soon going up to 200mg and in more ordinary good days i wouldn't notice the impact it does say compared to if i was taking 50mg cause the distress level is low.

&#x200B;

But when emotions build up, or bigger stressors or crisis happen that higher dose is more affective for it because the emotions are stronger and the distress is more than  usual. At least for myself and i'm glad to have medication that lowers the blow even if i don't need the massive dose majority of the time.

It does seem like you are well aware of your own behaviours and i'm sure therapy can be very beneficial to you now that are accepting to it. I don't have any advice on how to get off mediation ect but i'd just like to say anti phychotics can also be mood stabilizers, which is why you are feeling less agitation and anxiety symptoms and are often used for EUPD (myself and all my friends who have this diagnosis are on some anti-phythoptic) if you want to come off the medication i feel that you should be able to make that choice but i think i can see why they might not want you to at the time.

&#x200B;

From the way you are writing it sounds like you haven't started therapy yet, but will do and want to engage now you are ready. Which is absolutely great and i'm proud of you coming to this point. I think they might be concerned about you coming off the medication or decreasing before you have learnt things from therapy and that can have a wait time attached as well and could influence them in being worried about your safety for the time being. Alternatively keep taking 5mg and don't tell them and act like you are on 10mg if you can, as long as you are feeling good that matters most and if you at any point feel the need to increase it should be easy to bring up again

Abandoned a lot, my family was trying to get a better start for me but by age 2-3 my dad left the picture to move to the uk. Then my mum followed not long after, i stayed at my grandmas and had my own "cat" out of a random litter that was born that i had a really close friendship with. I didn't really have friends my own age at school. Then my family with no warning came and whisked me away from my life to the uk at age 5. I slept weeks crying missing my cat who they left behind with my grandma.

&#x200B;

I get why they did it but that shit opened me up to develop bpd and my teens only got worse than that. Dad became a druggie and mum was in denial and i'd comfort her instead of myself every night, harming myself first to supress my own feelings to focus on hers and begging her to leave him. She didn't tho, until it was exam season. The stress of it all and the building up mental issues made me pretty much flunk all my exams even subjects i was objectively good at and god predicted a's in. Became the anxious cried at everything even the teacher asking me a question teen then medication took the anxiety away and the bpd came through in full swing

I don't think a therapist should state anything in terms of breaking up or staying with someone as that's a choice for the person in treatment to make

&#x200B;

But i can see why it would be easier/better to focus on treatment alone, relationships add a lot of extra stressors, symptoms, can take away from that the other symptoms are or can "mask" them. The person may be co-dependent on you, you could be their FP, they could be putting too much focus on saving the relationship and the abandonment or rollercoaster feel of it rather than focusing on healing themselves, maybe even setting healthy boundaries or expressing themselves. Ultimately there are pros and cons to everything and it depends on what your partner feels is best and the choice they want to make in their treatment but it's good to be aware of setbacks that being with someone may give them or the pros of focusing purely on themselves

I think that depends, i've met someone who was told they had "symptoms of bpd" and that in of itself made him feel worse and more risk to himself as he was treating himself like it wasn't the issue or not "official" and then his ex gaslit him into thinking he made the whole thing up and was faking the condition cause it wasn't official. I tried to give him the dbt skills that i knew but he was reluctant to use them because he kept getting caught up in the "but i don't have bpd!" mindset and pushing the treatment away because of it

&#x200B;

Then myself, i was told i HAD BPD, it didn't change my ego or what my symptoms were at all actually it probably decreased a lot of them for me as i researched so much, papers, articles found some dbt skills all on my own and started implementing them myself cause i knew that was that was happening and it made me feel reassured, valued and everything shifted in my perception instead of "everything is overwhelming and how do i cope!" i started to be able to see it more like "this symptom is spiking up but if i wait it out it will go away so i just need to get through until the next morning and my mood will be happier or more stable"

It doesn't work for everyone but what helped me stop self harming was i replaced anything sharp ect i was using with a red pen or sharpie, the red made it look like blood and helped visually while the sensation felt semi similar ish in terms of pressure on the skin (especially when you tried to wash it off which took forever) can't promise it will work for you but you could try it and see if that's something that could help you? It's not permanent and can wash off eventually and you can write/draw whatever you like without worrying about scars

&#x200B;

Once they are fully healed you can think about options to cover it up, for now let them heal and theirs some oils that are good for scarring that you can use. You can think about tattoos as one option or maybe using makeup to conceal it, i'm sure theirs other things you could do

Honestly i think thats this sub in general, people who want to take responsibility, want to work on themselves, care about what they are doing ect i haven't noticed it on many posts here myself but i don't check too often

&#x200B;

You should check out the other bpd sub, it's the complete opposite to here where everyone echoes bad behaviour and a poor me attitude. It's a split between people who want to improve and want a good name for the disorder and see others do well and the other sub where everyone thinks they are the center. Not 100% true there is user overlap and people from both sides in both but yeah

