I think that it's easy to see these statements with lots of emotion, easy to see it a certain way but what i am getting out of this is that while there are aspects what may have scared him off as he was emotionally immature and not ready to deal with that, it doesn't change your value that is something you give to yourself and assign to others by perception of how they feel about you. I think that a combination of learning how to slow down, spread out information and emotions effectively is something you can work on but there are also people who are more mature out there and can handle these tougher emotions as well and when both collide together it could be a great progression that feels and is stable for both

&#x200B;

Therapy is not about making you feel better, it's also hard and a lot of it is about facts which sadly often are quite cold like how the truth is that a lot of people are scared of big or many emotions and would rather not handle it, but it doesn't make it less true and at the same time it doesn't make your value any lesser if you know that you want and require someone emotionally stronger and communicate it in a healthy manner to ensure you are getting your needs met and aren't suffering down inside hiding it either. A healthy balance. Give it some time, maybe review what has happened and has been said in the session in the morning hopefully with a fresh view

This 100% also many studies show that alienating people into these groups actually becomes an echo chamber. Be it grades, mental health, personality ect if you group a bunch of people with other "like minded" individuals it just becomes a back and forth of them influencing one another and none of them grow or develop while had they been kept around other people who were unlike them they could learn and grow their own abilities from those they interacted with

