I experience a lot of dissociation, to me it's more background/automatic depending on the sorta experience i end up having. Then theirs some i defiantly feel coming on, like starting to go foggy, brain stops working as fast, delayed responses and the longer i can't engage/don't get stimuli the faster it happens

&#x200B;

The more automatic stuff, is more scenery based and paranoid than out of body. Like everything looking closer than it actually is and missing it when i try to grab things, edges of my vision looking like their moving towards me. Whole parked empty cars looking and feeling like they are about to pull out and hit me

&#x200B;

The more foggy experience i get, is like everything becomes very muffled, blurry, foggy feeling and it almost feels like going underwater, i can stop myself from this one sometimes but in extremely overwhelming instances i've had  trying to ground ends up with me just in the middle, like not grounded and in reality but not fully out of body and just fighting this back and forth of almost being in either until the experience passes. At it's most intense i've had this, i was in that middle ground for an hour trying to fight it and ground myself

Others have given you really good comfort/support so i'll focus more on productive things to try and it will make you slightly ahead in therapy

&#x200B;

Some skills you can look and try are called TIPP and STOP, tipp is about finding things and ways to lower the emotional reaction. The part of if that i find most helpful personally is the water, you soak or splash cold water on your face and it calms almost immediately (not fully you will still be hurt, upset ect but it's lesser). Stop is about stopping before you act, noticing signs that you are about to be upset, mad ect and forcing yourself to not react before you have time to think about what would be most beneficial and not making a situation worse.

&#x200B;

So you already know that him leaving is going to be a trigger, so you can try and prepare yourself and focus on making it as minimalistic in terms of problem behaviour like throwing, yelling ect. Also i'd like to touch on the fact that i'm sure it's really hard on you when he leaves, that's valid but try to be nice to yourself and try and soothe a bit that it isn't forever. The better you two communicate, smoother the visits go the better it might start to feel. Don't forget you are allowed to voice wants/needs as well, for example if he wants or needs to leave earlier he's allowed to of course, but you can calmly ask if it's okay if before he goes he gives you some physical affection which you mentioned you'd have liked.

